{"title":"Pentadecagon","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>A <b>pentadecagon</b> is a polygon with 15 sides. Each of its interior angles are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/f/50ff9540650f0f95f80da2f6692fc8ee413e2eb3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$144$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"27\" height=\"12\" />, while each of their exterior angles are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/9/e290f2cba0ba0e11c0b91630da15a408da2fcf1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$24$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<ol><li>Pentadecagon</li></ol>\n<p>A **pentadecagon** is a polygon with **15 sides**. The name comes from Greek roots: \"penta\" meaning 5 and \"deca\" meaning 10, so \"pentadecagon\" literally means 15-sided polygon.\n</p><p>---\n</p>\n<hr />\n<ol><li><ol><li>1. Basic Properties</li></ol></li></ol>\n<p>- Number of sides: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/a/d5a242689b4cdcd8be0c39edc9a19ed508070de3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 15$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"52\" height=\"13\" />  \n- Number of vertices: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/e/68ef523e09bc83debdcd4e55ceff996e6f498333.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v = 15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"54\" height=\"14\" />  \n- Number of diagonals: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/0/af0c0dd24b68f56c9f3f273aba2eaaec333e83d6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n(n-3)}{2} = \\frac{15 \\cdot 12}{2} = 90$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"193\" height=\"40\" />  \n- Sum of interior angles: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/b/3fbd0b99f4681ecbceeaa043a0ffbaced5dc3937.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n-2) \\cdot 180 = 13 \\cdot 180 = 2340^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"250\" height=\"20\" />  \n- Each interior angle (regular pentadecagon): <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/d/3cdc40bd26fe70badbe64f6b22d4ca0f0ad682c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2340}{15} = 156^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"101\" height=\"38\" />  \n- Each exterior angle: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/6/f36d38f41b617eaae299eac5ff99fc660eb76237.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180 - 156 = 24^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"129\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>&gt; **Note:** The sum of exterior angles of any polygon is always <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/9/cf9506d61661a7d046ee93b381ddb704684ffd15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$360^\\circ$\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p>\n<hr />\n<ol><li><ol><li>2. Interior and Exterior Angles</li></ol></li></ol>\n<p>- Interior angle formula: \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/b/82bbd02f7efc399c30502d2ca011ce42c36f4e0c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Interior angle} = \\frac{(n-2) \\cdot 180}{n}\\]\" width=\"221\" height=\"38\" />\n- Exterior angle formula: \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/6/62681fab548a800869d4b22dcfcab5a8ef202c52.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Exterior angle} = 180 - \\text{Interior angle} = \\frac{360}{n}\\]\" width=\"328\" height=\"37\" />\n- For a pentadecagon:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/0/1b0431a5c0b793bd4985bc8298460fac4ff06893.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Interior angle} = \\frac{(15-2)\\cdot180}{15} = 156^\\circ\\]\" width=\"287\" height=\"38\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/d/2ad85b0f08278436a04c56a2d8c888ae9eea38ce.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Exterior angle} = 180 - 156 = 24^\\circ\\]\" width=\"251\" height=\"16\" />\n</p>\n<hr />\n<ol><li><ol><li>3. Diagonals</li></ol></li></ol>\n<p>- The total number of diagonals in a polygon is given by:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/d/1dd099020e0c4e371dcc00e3df3cc8b83cb47bbb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[d = \\frac{n(n-3)}{2}\\]\" width=\"102\" height=\"38\" />\n- For a pentadecagon:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/5/9d5dedb2a8a62257eb2b772fffbcbcdd23987be6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[d = \\frac{15 \\cdot 12}{2} = 90\\]\" width=\"128\" height=\"38\" />\n- **Observation:** A diagonal connects two non-adjacent vertices.\n</p>\n<hr />\n<ol><li><ol><li>4. Area of a Regular Pentadecagon</li></ol></li></ol>\n<p>For a regular pentadecagon with side length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, the area can be calculated as:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/7/3e7704277f425348d6af34059bf6c7c1653f0c0e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[A = \\frac{15}{4} a^2 \\cot \\frac{\\pi}{15}\\]\" width=\"127\" height=\"38\" />\n- This formula comes from dividing the pentadecagon into 15 congruent isosceles triangles with vertex at the center.\n</p>\n<hr />\n<ol><li><ol><li>5. Symmetry</li></ol></li></ol>\n<p>- A regular pentadecagon has **rotational symmetry of order 15**.  \n- It has **15 lines of reflection symmetry**, one passing through each vertex and the midpoint of the opposite side.\n</p>\n<hr />\n<ol><li><ol><li>6. Circumcircle and Incircle</li></ol></li></ol>\n<p>- All vertices of a regular pentadecagon lie on a **circumcircle**.  \n- The radius of the circumcircle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is related to side <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> by:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/e/f1e7ef9aa33ebb0e291943f75188670dc0394efc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[R = \\frac{a}{2 \\sin (\\pi/15)}\\]\" width=\"126\" height=\"37\" />\n- The radius of the incircle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/4/b84b8c5a46c159e230a722032f19dd4ef8fc0703.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[r = \\frac{a}{2 \\tan (\\pi/15)}\\]\" width=\"125\" height=\"37\" />\n</p>\n<hr />\n<ol><li><ol><li>7. Constructibility</li></ol></li></ol>\n<p>- A regular pentadecagon is **constructible with compass and straightedge** because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/8/d28549e304f70a3adf743c6ce7ec501a792e16be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15 = 3 \\cdot 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"76\" height=\"14\" />, and both 3-gon (triangle) and 5-gon (pentagon) are constructible.\n</p>\n<hr />\n<ol><li><ol><li>8. Angle Measures Summary</li></ol></li></ol>\n<p>| Angle Type         | Measure      |\n|-------------------|-------------|\n| Interior angle     | <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/c/b5cecd462fa627de9315e0219e1b66c1716185c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$156^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"37\" height=\"15\" /> |\n| Exterior angle     | <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/f/15fdc5e95bd0d52233b70706367b2a523c6336af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$24^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" />  |\n| Central angle      | <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/f/15fdc5e95bd0d52233b70706367b2a523c6336af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$24^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" />  |\n| Sum of interior    | <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/d/ccddcaafb4b3c66a64f5d7e824a874e6f1b3babb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2340^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" />|\n| Sum of exterior    | <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/9/cf9506d61661a7d046ee93b381ddb704684ffd15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$360^\\circ$\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" /> |\n</p>\n<hr />\n<ol><li><ol><li>9. Applications</li></ol></li></ol>\n<p>- Pentadecagons appear in tiling patterns and architectural designs.  \n- They can also appear in **recreational mathematics problems** involving symmetry and combinatorics.  \n</p>\n<hr />\n<ol><li><ol><li>10. Examples</li></ol></li></ol>\n<p>1. If each side of a regular pentadecagon is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> units, its area is:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/7/86748103fe7ee7059b1d801d78c650b18457f702.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[A = \\frac{15}{4} \\cdot 5^2 \\cot \\frac{\\pi}{15} \\approx 181.69 \\text{ units}^2\\]\" width=\"264\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>2. Number of diagonals that intersect at a single point inside a regular pentadecagon can be calculated using combinatorial methods.  \n-\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>A pentadecagon is a **beautifully symmetric polygon**, rich with properties connecting geometry, trigonometry, and algebra. Understanding its angles, diagonals, and symmetry is useful in advanced polygon studies.\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=~Trashboycan\" title=\"~Trashboycan\">~Trashboycan</a>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018102844\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.017 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.029 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 91/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 5528/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:9251:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 202610181028